WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.920
Alice Krige might be playing the Witch in
Gretel &amp; Hansel, but the horror-tinged reimagining

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.019
of the classic fairy tale isn't the first
time that the veteran actress has played an

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:10.160
iconic baddie.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.180
In 1996, Krige appeared in Star Trek: First
Contact as the villainous Borg Queen, a role

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.640
she reprised in Star Trek: Voyager and a couple
of spin-off projects.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.940
With the Borg playing a significant role in
Jean-Luc Picard's latest adventures in the

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.590
CBS All Access series Star Trek: Picard, we
thought it'd be interesting to ask Krige about

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.660
her time as the former Starfleet captain's
arch-nemesis.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.770
While talking with Looper about her role in
Gretel &amp; Hansel, Krige shared her thoughts

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.780
on what it takes to bring a memorable villain
to life, saying,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.580
"I'm interested in what it is to be human
and to be alive.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.380
Your job as an actor is to try to bring the
humanness, the frailty, the bit that we all

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.180
recognize."

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.300
However, when it came to playing Star Trek's
Borg Queen, finding the humanity in the character

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.960
presented some difficulties, since, as Krige
explained,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.880
"I don't think she is human.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.380
No one has told me this; this is what I have
come to myself, [but] I think she was an entity

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.480
that happened at the moment of creation.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.170
And she's always been that and she always
will be, and it is entirely abnormal."

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.140
"Human.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.240
We used to be exactly like them.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.020
Flawed, weak, organic."

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.580
In addition, when it comes to larger-than-life
roles like the Borg Queen or Gretel &amp; Hansel's

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.620
Witch, Krige noted that it can make the task
of grounding those characters particularly

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.620
challenging, saying,

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.480
"You are allowed to live life through those
characters as large as it is possible to write

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.520
it [...] They're archetypes.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:34.680
Somehow they crystallize or distill the human
condition [...] It's about catharsis, about

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.900
the insoluble problem of being human."

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.940
On the other hand, villainous parts do provide
Krige with a sense of freedom.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.640
As the actress revealed to Looper,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.660
"With other roles, you need to try and be
just like someone you'd meet in the street.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.540
You're not going to meet [the Witch or the
Borg Queen] in the street.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.370
So every now and then, to be given the chance
to live in the magic forest is wonderful because

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:58.930
it crystallizes on certain levels what we
all face as human beings in a very, very bold,

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.170
in-your-face kind of way."

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.900
But even though Krige seems to have a good
handle on her dark characters, playing villains

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.780
still can take a severe mental toll.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.159
As an actress, Krige has to live inside her
characters' heads for weeks, or sometimes

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:10.840
even months.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.689
When she's playing characters like the Witch
and the Borg Queen, that's not necessarily

00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:15.620
a nice place to be.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.529
In fact, Krige almost didn't accept the role
of the Witch in Gretel &amp; Hansel because she

00:02:20.529 --> 00:02:23.020
was wary of spending so much time in the dark.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:28.280
In 2006, Krige played a cult leader named
Christabella in Silent Hill, and it left quite

00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:29.489
a mark on her psyche.

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:31.950
Speaking of her experience on Silent Hill,
Krige said,

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.799
"I didn't anticipate with Christabella how
dark she would be.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.140
You take something on, and you can't do half-measures.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.240
You have to go inside that space, right?

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.700
And the last few weeks it was just Christabella
mad and burning people.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.620
It was a dark space to go to."

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.860
"That is where the darkness lies.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.860
The demon is in the bowels of this building.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.760
It hides behind the face of an innocent."

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.440
Thankfully for her own well-being, the actress
has since emerged from that dark space.

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:01.500
Thanks to Gretel &amp; Hansel director Osgood
Perkins' gentle touch, Krige felt totally

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.189
comfortable inhabiting her latest villainous
role.

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:06.239
Speaking about Perkins' approach, Krige said,

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.840
"I knew that he would come to the Witch without
judgment, and that he would illuminate who

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:13.590
she was and what she became with understanding
and compassion.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.689
Osgood was always looking at her point of
view, with her pain, with her compulsion,

00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:20.019
her grief, her shame, her exhaustion."

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.059
Ultimately, that human perspective is what
helped separate the Witch from the Borg Queen

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:24.680
in Krige's mind.

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:25.960
As the actress noted,

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:31.100
"By the end [of Gretel &amp; Hansel] you had a
human being who didn't start off in this place,

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.520
but who wound up in this place.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.360
Sometimes we make decisions all alone, and
we live with them for the rest of our lives.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.740
And that's what happened to the Witch."

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.060
That's a far cry from Krige's take on the
non-human Borg Queen.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.680
Looking back on her iconic Star Trek role,
Krige said,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.620
"I wouldn't like to spend my life inside the
Borg Queen, but it was a great adventure to

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.420
live inside her."

